Purpose: Reverse-engineer Wave API specs from client analysis

This experiment establishes a systematic approach to documenting the Wave protocol API
by analyzing client-side JavaScript code and creating comprehensive OpenAPI 3.0 specifications.

Goal: Create comprehensive OpenAPI 3.0 specification for Wave protocol
- Analyze Wave client JavaScript for API patterns and endpoints
- Document authentication, session management, and WebSocket protocols
- Create machine-readable API specifications with examples and schemas

Expected areas:
- Session management (authentication, tokens, user sessions)
- Wavelet operations (create, read, update, delete wavelets)
- Blip CRUD operations (create, modify, delete blips and content)
- WebSocket protocol (real-time updates, operational transforms)
- User management (participants, permissions, presence)
- File operations (attachments, media handling)

Output: wave-api.openapi.yaml with schemas, examples, and documentation
- Complete OpenAPI 3.0 specification
- Request/response schemas with validation
- Example requests and responses
- Comprehensive documentation of all endpoints

Dependency: Builds on findings from experiment 002 client analysis
- Uses client code analysis from experiment 002
- Leverages understanding of Wave protocol internals
- Maps client-side API calls to server-side specifications

FPO workflow: Placeholder structure for systematic API documentation
- Makefile provides reproducible documentation generation
- README establishes methodology and standards
- Framework for iterative specification development
